    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>From periodic sampling to operational intelligence</h2>

      <p>
        Wood-chip quality affects process stability, product consistency, and
        energy efficiency in pulp, paper, and bioenergy manufacturing.
        Conventional assessment commonly depends on periodic manual sampling
        and offline measurements, limiting how quickly operators can respond to
        changes in chip geometry or moisture condition.
      </p>

      <p>
        Wood-Chip Monitor was developed as a portable edge-AI platform that
        moves visual quality assessment closer to the production environment.
        It transforms a live RGB stream into physical measurements,
        population-level statistics, quality alerts, and operator-facing
        information without requiring cloud inference for the primary
        inspection workflow.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>End-to-end system architecture</h2>

      <p>
        The platform connects three layers. The acquisition layer captures
        streaming RGB imagery using the portable camera system. The inference
        layer executes TensorRT-optimized geometry and moisture models on
        NVIDIA Jetson hardware. The operational layer converts the resulting
        measurements into rolling statistics, size distributions, oversize
        alarms, moisture summaries, live annotations, and configurable quality
        controls.
      </p>

      <p>
        This design moves beyond a model-only demonstration by connecting
        computer vision with calibration, physical measurement, data
        persistence, backend services, device monitoring, and a browser-based
        operator interface.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Geometry and moisture intelligence</h2>

      <p>
        The geometry engine uses a DETR-based detector to localize individual
        chips in dense and overlapping scenes. Scene calibration converts
        image-space detections into physical dimensions, which are aggregated
        into rolling size-distribution statistics and evaluated against
        configurable oversize thresholds. The associated UOT-DETR research
        introduces a distributionally constrained training objective to
        improve recovery of the observed chip population.
      </p>

      <p>
        The moisture engine uses MoistNetLite, a lightweight vision model, to
        classify selected chip regions as dry, medium, or wet. A configurable
        Top-K strategy limits moisture inference to representative
        high-confidence detections, bounding the computational cost in dense
        scenes while preserving a live frame-level moisture summary.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Selected outcomes</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>Geometry-engine mAR50: 95%</li>
        <li>Reported size-distribution error: KL = 0.04</li>
        <li>Moisture-classification accuracy: 91%</li>
        <li>TensorRT-optimized execution on an NVIDIA Jetson edge device</li>
        <li>Local browser dashboard for live monitoring and quality control</li>

      </ul>

      <p>
        Together, these components demonstrate the complete translation of
        research methods into a portable intelligent system for real-time
        manufacturing decision support.
      </p>
    </section>

